<HowToGuide title="How to Create a DT:FC Children&rsquo;s Script" currentGuide="create-a-script">
  <p>
    DT:FC plays work like magic &mdash; but not the kind with elaborate special effects or fancy
    costumes. Success comes from something much more powerful: the children&rsquo;s imaginations!
  </p>

  <h2>Less is more: keep it simple and be fearlessly creative</h2>
  <p>
    Simple doesn&rsquo;t mean boring &mdash; it means clear and powerful. DT:FC plays are performed
    on a bare stage, with neither sets nor props, and minimal costumes. Minimal props and sets mean
    maximum imagination.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Choose the story &mdash; follow the plot</h2>
  <p>
    The beginning is to decide on an old story, a parable, maybe a favourite of yours, that you
    want to transform into a play. Almost <em>any</em> traditional story can be transformed. The
    story provides the plot &mdash; the sequence of events. Keep the plot simple.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Playmaking is in the oral tradition</h2>
  <p>
    The goal is to capture the audience&rsquo;s complete attention by accessing their imagination
    through their ears. Clarity is an important element in the communication process.
  </p>
  <p>The oral tradition uses:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Repetition</strong> of important information aids clarity. Repetition takes many forms:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <em>Scene repeats:</em> there are three children, and so three scenes where the first two
          times the characters fail, but the third child succeeds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <em>Theme repeats:</em> various characters refer to the goal or other main point.
        </li>
        <li><em>Line repeats,</em> including songs.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Careful selection of action and effect</strong> strikes chords of memory and experience.
      <ul>
        <li>
          The plot is a Wayfarer&rsquo;s Journey: the same basic elements occur in every story.
          See the{' '}
          <a href="/childrens-theatre/how-to/archetype-of-one-story/">Archetype of One Story</a>{' '}
          guide for the full pattern.
        </li>
        <li>
          The characters are archetypes: hero/heroine, villain, bad or good parent, wise helper&hellip;
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Speech followed by action followed by speech followed by action.</strong> This pattern
      allows the audience time to digest each unit of spoken or observed information. Stories should
      flow naturally like the ebb and tide.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Invite wonder with strangeness</h2>
  <p>
    Strange and exotic invites curiosity and awakens the mind. Set the story in unfamiliar places
    &mdash; it immediately captures attention. Children love exploring new worlds through story.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Invite audience participation</h2>
  <p>
    Involve the audience directly. They will provide sound effects, spontaneously or by being asked
    by the Players. At times the audience is directly invited to make choices for the characters.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Handle difficult themes thoughtfully</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Children understand the difference between theatrical reality and everyday life.</li>
    <li>
      Instead of removing challenging elements from fairy tales &mdash; the reason the story exists
      is to transmit how to survive the problem and thrive.
    </li>
    <li>Violence is never for entertainment &mdash; always stylised and essential to the plot.</li>
    <li>These stories help children understand their own capacity to overcome challenges.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Let children make it their own</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The best plays live on in children&rsquo;s imaginations and games.</li>
    <li>
      Resist the urge to add a moral at the end &mdash; let children discover meaning themselves.
    </li>
    <li>Trust children to find what matters to them in the story.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    DT:FC playmaking isn&rsquo;t about impressing audiences with flashy effects or non-stop action.
    It&rsquo;s about making theatrical moments that resonate deeply. When you invite the audience to
    use their imagination, you&rsquo;re asking them to bring themselves into the production &mdash;
    making it <em>their</em> story too!
  </p>
  <p>
    Each audience person risks becoming committed to the production in the hopes that something
    useful will be learned through the theatrical experience. When that learning occurs, the person
    feels pleasure &mdash; they risked, they learned something, they feel good about it.
  </p>
  <p>
    So choose your story and start playing! Remember the DT:FC principle: &ldquo;Say Yes,
    and&hellip;&rdquo; Your creativity is limitless, and the children&rsquo;s imaginations love
    explorations.
  </p>
</HowToGuide>
